IMPORT prelude

-- Minimum Conditions of Employment Act 1993 (WA) -- what a State system employee meets.
--
-- SEAT: the employee. "Does this Act apply to me at all, and if it does, what is the least my employer must give me?"
-- Every question below is phrased as that person would ask it, on facts that person could state.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED. Who the Act reaches: s 3(1) "employee" and "employer" (borrowed from the Industrial Relations
-- Act 1979 s 7(1) and s 7A, asked as facts), s 5 (binds all employers and employees; no contracting out; no waiver),
-- s 6A (police officers); with the Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth) s 26 exclusion asked as a fact (see below). Cashing out
-- annual leave (s 8); maximum hours (s 9A); minimum rates of pay (ss 10, 11, 12, 13, 14); personal leave (ss 19, 20,
-- 20A, 21); paid annual leave (ss 23, 24, 25, 26); paid bereavement leave (s 27, with the s 3 "member of the
-- employee's family or household"); public holidays (ss 30, 31); unpaid family and domestic violence leave (ss 39B,
-- 39C); the right to be told of a redundancy and paid leave for job interviews (ss 40, 41, 43).
--
-- Edition: the consolidated text at legislation.wa.gov.au (law_a516, document mrdoc_48248, retrieved 7 September 2026;
-- amendments shown up to No. 43 of 2024), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_48248.txt.
-- Also read (not encoded): Industrial Relations Act 1979 (WA) ss 7(1), 7A, 50A, as deposited at
-- ../../../industrial-relations-act-1979/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49816.txt; Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth) s 26, as
-- deposited at ../../../../fair-work-act-2009/registers/source-bundle/C2009A00028.txt.
-- No regulations are used: the money figures come from the annual State Wage order made under the IR Act s 50A, which
-- is not deposited, so the minimum weekly rates are INPUTS.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Employees with disabilities and the Supported Wage System (ss 15-17); the pay-method rules of Part 3A
-- (ss 17A-17E); rates of pay for leave (ss 18, 18A, 18B) beyond what the hours questions need; compensated employees;
-- substituted public holidays (s 32) and leave over a public holiday (s 33); proof of entitlement (ss 22A, 28, 39D);
-- confidentiality (s 39E); flexible working arrangement requests (Part 4A); the transitional Parts 8 and 9;
-- enforcement (s 7, the IR Act s 83) and the regulation power (s 47) as machinery. The Act contains NO notice of
-- termination and NO redundancy pay: neither appears in the deposited text.
--
-- BASELINE. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the design defaults ruled on
-- 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice.
-- See NOTES.md.

§ `Minimum Conditions of Employment Act 1993 -- the employee's questions`

§§ `ss 3, 5, 6A -- does this Act apply to me`

-- s 3(1): "employee has the meaning given in the IR Act section 7(1)". IR Act s 7(1): "an individual who is employed
-- by an employer to do work for hire or reward, including as an apprentice"; IR Act s 7A(2): decided by "the real
-- substance, practical reality and true nature of the relationship". A borrowed term, so asked as facts.
-- s 6A: "This Act applies to a police officer as if (a) the police officer were an employee".
-- s 5(1): "The minimum conditions of employment extend to and bind all employees and employers".
-- The Act itself says nothing about national system employers except in s 3 "paid family and domestic violence leave"
-- ("a non-national system employee") and the Notes to Part 4 and Division 7. The exclusion is the Fair Work Act 2009
-- s 26(1): it "is intended to apply to the exclusion of all State or Territory industrial laws so far as they would
-- otherwise apply in relation to a national system employee or a national system employer"; s 26(2)(b)(ii) and (c)
-- reach a State Act that applies to employment generally and provides for "terms and conditions of employment" or
-- "deals with leave". READING (an inference, not stated in either Act): this Act is such a law, so it gives nothing
-- to anyone whose employer is a national system employer. Whether the employer is one (FW Act s 14) is asked as a fact.
DECLARE MyWork HAS
    `I work for someone for hire or reward` IS A BOOLEAN
    `in substance the relationship is employment, not contracting` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a police officer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `my employer is a national system employer` IS A BOOLEAN

@export Am I an employee for this Act (s 3(1), IR Act ss 7(1) and 7A; s 6A)?
GIVEN `w` IS A MyWork @desc Your work: who you work for and on what footing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an employee for this Act` IF
        (`w`'s `I work for someone for hire or reward` AND `w`'s `in substance the relationship is employment, not contracting`)
    OR  `w`'s `I am a police officer`

@export Does this Act give me its minimum conditions (s 5, read with Fair Work Act s 26)?
GIVEN `w` IS A MyWork @desc Your work: who you work for and on what footing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this Act give me its minimum conditions` IF
        `Am I an employee for this Act` `w`
    AND (NOT `w`'s `my employer is a national system employer`)

-- s 5(2): "A provision in, or condition of, an industrial instrument or a contract of employment that is less
-- favourable to the employee than a minimum condition of employment has no effect"; (4) "A purported waiver of a right
-- under this Act has no effect"; (5) "This section has effect subject to section 8" (cashing out annual leave).
@export Does a term of my contract or award that gives me less than a minimum condition bind me (s 5)?
GIVEN `the term is less favourable to me than a minimum condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term gives you less than this Act's minimum
      `the term is a valid agreement to cash out annual leave under s 8` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term is an agreement to cash out annual leave that meets s 8
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the term bind me` IF
        (NOT `the term is less favourable to me than a minimum condition`)
    OR  `the term is a valid agreement to cash out annual leave under s 8`

§§ `s 8 -- cashing out annual leave`

-- s 8(1): "After the completion of any year of service ... the employer and employee may agree that the employee may
-- forgo taking paid annual leave ... if (a) the amount ... forgone does not exceed 50% of the whole amount ... (b) the
-- employee is given an equivalent benefit ... (c) the agreement is in writing, signed by the employer and the
-- employee." (2) not equivalent "unless the employee is paid at least the amount the employee would have been paid".
-- (2B) a later agreement counts "the total amount ... forgone under all agreements". (2C) no effect if "the
-- employer's offer of employment was made on the condition that the employee would be required to enter into the
-- agreement".
@export Is my agreement to cash out annual leave valid (s 8)?
GIVEN `the year of service is complete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the year of service the leave belongs to has been completed
      `hours forgone under all agreements for that year` IS A NUMBER @desc Total hours of leave given up for that year, this agreement included
      `hours of annual leave earned in that year` IS A NUMBER @desc Hours of paid annual leave you became entitled to for that year
      `I am paid at least what the leave would have paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payment is at least what you would have been paid for taking the leave
      `it is in writing signed by both of us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement is written and signed by you and your employer
      `my job offer was conditional on agreeing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer made signing it a condition of the job offer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my cash-out agreement valid` IF
        `the year of service is complete`
    AND `hours forgone under all agreements for that year` AT MOST (`hours of annual leave earned in that year` TIMES 0.5)
    AND `I am paid at least what the leave would have paid`
    AND `it is in writing signed by both of us`
    AND (NOT `my job offer was conditional on agreeing`)

§§ `ss 9A, 9B -- maximum hours`

-- s 9A(1): "An employee is not to be required or requested by an employer to work more than (a) either (i) the
-- employee's ordinary hours of work as specified in an industrial instrument ... or (ii) if there is no industrial
-- instrument that specifies the employee's ordinary hours of work, 38 hours per week; and (b) reasonable additional
-- hours as determined under section 9B." (2) hours worked "include any hours of authorised leave". Whether extra hours
-- are reasonable is a judgment on the s 9B factors: asked as a fact.
@export What are my ordinary weekly hours for the maximum-hours rule (s 9A(1)(a))?
GIVEN `an award or agreement sets my ordinary hours` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an industrial instrument covering you specifies your ordinary hours
      `the ordinary hours it sets` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly ordinary hours it specifies (ignored if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What are my ordinary weekly hours` IS
    IF `an award or agreement sets my ordinary hours` THEN `the ordinary hours it sets` ELSE 38

@export Can I be required or asked to work these hours this week (s 9A)?
GIVEN `hours this week, counting authorised leave` IS A NUMBER @desc Hours you would work this week, with any authorised leave counted as hours worked
      `my ordinary weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc Your ordinary weekly hours under s 9A(1)(a)
      `if the extra hours are reasonable under s 9B` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For hours beyond ordinary: whether they are reasonable, taking account of safety, family responsibilities, the business, notice and the last 4 weeks' hours
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be asked to work these hours` IF
        `hours this week, counting authorised leave` AT MOST `my ordinary weekly hours`
    OR  `if the extra hours are reasonable under s 9B`

§§ `ss 10-14 -- minimum rates of pay`

-- s 12: for an employee "who has reached 21 years of age" and "is not an apprentice", "the rate in effect at that time
-- under section 50A(1)(a)(i) of the IR Act" (the State Wage order: not deposited, an INPUT).
-- s 13: under 21, "the percentage, set out opposite that age ... of the rate referred to in section 12 ... rounded to
-- the nearest 10 cents": 20 years 90%; 19 years 80%; 18 years 70%; 17 years 60%; 16 years 50%; under 16 years 40%.
-- s 14: an apprentice gets the IR Act s 50A(1)(a)(ii) rate for the class of apprentice or apprentices generally (an
-- INPUT).
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`percentage of the adult rate for my age` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 21 THEN 100
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 20 THEN 90
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 19 THEN 80
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 18 THEN 70
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 17 THEN 60
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 16 THEN 50
        OTHERWISE 40

@export What is my minimum weekly rate of pay (ss 12, 13, 14)?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `I am an apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an apprentice
      `the State Wage adult rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly rate for adults who are not apprentices, set by the current State Wage order (IR Act s 50A(1)(a)(i))
      `the State Wage apprentice rate for me` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly rate for your class of apprentice, or apprentices generally, set by the State Wage order (IR Act s 50A(1)(a)(ii))
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is my minimum weekly rate` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `I am an apprentice` THEN `the State Wage apprentice rate for me`
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 21 THEN `the State Wage adult rate`
        OTHERWISE (ROUND (`the State Wage adult rate` TIMES `percentage of the adult rate for my age` `my age` DIVIDED BY 10)) DIVIDED BY 10

-- s 10(1): "an employee is entitled to be paid, for each hour worked ... the minimum weekly rate of pay applicable
-- ... divided by 38". s 11: "A casual employee is entitled to be paid the amount ... under section 10 plus the
-- prescribed percentage of that amount"; prescribed percentage "(a) 25%; or (b) if a percentage higher than 25% is set
-- by order under section 51I of the IR Act ..., that percentage". (s 16 takes Supported Wage employees out of s 10:
-- not encoded.)
@export What is my minimum hourly rate of pay (ss 10, 11)?
GIVEN `my minimum weekly rate` IS A NUMBER @desc Your minimum weekly rate under s 12, 13 or 14
      `I am casual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a casual employee (decided on the real substance of the relationship, IR Act s 7B)
      `a higher casual loading set by order, if any` IS A NUMBER @desc A casual loading percentage above 25% set by order under IR Act s 51I; 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is my minimum hourly rate` IS
    IF `I am casual`
    THEN `my minimum weekly rate` DIVIDED BY 38 TIMES (100 PLUS (max 25 `a higher casual loading set by order, if any`)) DIVIDED BY 100
    ELSE `my minimum weekly rate` DIVIDED BY 38

§§ `ss 19, 20, 20A, 21 -- personal leave`

-- s 20(1): "For each year of service, an employee (other than a casual employee) is entitled to paid personal leave
-- for the number of hours the employee is ordinarily required to work in a 2-week period during that year, up to a
-- maximum of 76 hours." (2) "accrues pro rata on a weekly basis" and "is cumulative".
@export How many hours of paid personal leave do I earn each year (s 20)?
GIVEN `I am casual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a casual employee
      `my ordinary weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours you are ordinarily required to work each week
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many hours of personal leave a year` IS
    IF `I am casual` THEN 0 ELSE min 76 (`my ordinary weekly hours` TIMES 2)

-- s 20A(1): "An employee may take paid personal leave if the employee is unable to work as a result of personal
-- circumstances." (2) not paid for own illness or injury "attributable to ... (a) the employee's serious and wilful
-- misconduct; (b) the employee's gross and wilful neglect" in the course of employment. s 19: personal circumstances
-- are illness or injury of the employee or a family or household member, or "an unexpected emergency" affecting a
-- member. READING: the employee must have accrued hours to draw on (s 20); asked as a fact.
@export Am I paid for this absence as personal leave (s 20A)?
GIVEN `I cannot work because of illness, injury or a family emergency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you cannot work because of your own or a family or household member's illness or injury, or an unexpected emergency affecting a member
      `it is my own illness or injury caused by my serious and wilful misconduct or gross and wilful neglect at work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is your own illness or injury and was caused by your serious and wilful misconduct or gross and wilful neglect in your job
      `I have enough accrued personal leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have enough paid personal leave accrued
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I paid for this personal leave` IF
        `I cannot work because of illness, injury or a family emergency`
    AND (NOT `it is my own illness or injury caused by my serious and wilful misconduct or gross and wilful neglect at work`)
    AND `I have enough accrued personal leave`

-- s 21(1): "unpaid personal leave of up to 2 days for each occasion"; (2) only if "(a) the permissible occasion
-- comprises a circumstance referred to in paragraphs (a)(ii) and (b) of the definition of personal circumstances" (a
-- family or household member's illness or injury, or an unexpected emergency affecting one) and "(b) the employee
-- cannot take paid personal leave under section 20A".
@export How many days of unpaid personal leave can I take for this occasion (s 21)?
GIVEN `it is a family or household member's illness, injury or emergency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the occasion is a family or household member's illness or injury, or an unexpected emergency affecting one
      `I cannot take paid personal leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you cannot take paid personal leave (for example, a casual, or none accrued)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days of unpaid personal leave` IS
    IF `it is a family or household member's illness, injury or emergency` AND `I cannot take paid personal leave`
    THEN 2
    ELSE 0

§§ `ss 23-26 -- paid annual leave`

-- s 23(1): "For each year of service, an employee (other than a casual employee) is entitled to paid annual leave for
-- the number of hours the employee is ordinarily required to work in a 4-week period during that year, up to a maximum
-- of 152 hours." (2) "accrues pro rata on a weekly basis". s 3: "year of service does not include any period of
-- unpaid leave".
@export How many hours of paid annual leave do I earn each year (s 23)?
GIVEN `I am casual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a casual employee
      `my ordinary weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours you are ordinarily required to work each week
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many hours of annual leave a year` IS
    IF `I am casual` THEN 0 ELSE min 152 (`my ordinary weekly hours` TIMES 4)

@export How many hours of annual leave have I accrued (s 23(2))?
GIVEN `I am casual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a casual employee
      `my ordinary weekly hours` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours you are ordinarily required to work each week
      `weeks of service, not counting unpaid leave` IS A NUMBER @desc Weeks of service so far, leaving out unpaid leave
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many hours of annual leave accrued` IS
    `How many hours of annual leave a year` `I am casual` `my ordinary weekly hours` TIMES `weeks of service, not counting unpaid leave` DIVIDED BY 52

-- s 24(1): "If an employer and an employee have not agreed when the employee is to take paid annual leave, the
-- employer must not refuse the employee taking, at a time suitable to the employee, a period of paid annual leave the
-- employee became entitled to more than 12 months before that time." (2) "The employee is to give the employer at
-- least 2 weeks' notice". READING: (2) is treated as a condition of (1).
@export Can I insist on taking my annual leave at a time that suits me (s 24)?
GIVEN `my employer and I have agreed when I take it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and your employer have already agreed when you will take the leave
      `months since I became entitled to it` IS A NUMBER @desc How many months before the leave starts you became entitled to it
      `weeks of notice I give` IS A NUMBER @desc How many weeks' notice of the leave you give your employer
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I insist on my annual leave` IF
        (NOT `my employer and I have agreed when I take it`)
    AND `months since I became entitled to it` GREATER THAN 12
    AND `weeks of notice I give` AT LEAST 2

-- s 25(1): paid "at the time payment is made in the normal course of the employment"; (2) "However, if an employee
-- taking paid annual leave requests in writing, the employee must be paid for the leave before it starts."
@export Must I be paid for my annual leave before it starts (s 25)?
GIVEN `I asked in writing to be paid in advance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked your employer in writing to pay you before the leave starts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be paid before my leave starts` IF `I asked in writing to be paid in advance`

-- s 26(1): "An employee is entitled to be paid out for all untaken paid annual leave when the employee's employment
-- ends." (2) not for leave "that relates to a partly completed year of service if the employee's employment is ended
-- (a) unlawfully by the employee; or (b) by the employer because of the fault of the employee." (3) "Despite
-- subsection (2), if ... ended by the employer because of the employee's misconduct, the employee is not entitled to
-- be paid out for untaken paid annual leave that relates to a year of service completed after the misconduct."
-- READING: misconduct is a kind of fault, so the partly completed year is also lost under (2)(b).
DECLARE HowItEnded IS ONE OF
    `I resigned lawfully`
    `I walked out unlawfully`
    `my employer ended it, not for any fault of mine`
    `my employer ended it for my fault`
    `my employer ended it for my misconduct`

@export How many hours of untaken annual leave must I be paid out when my job ends (s 26)?
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A HowItEnded @desc How your employment ended
      `untaken hours from completed years` IS A NUMBER @desc Untaken annual leave hours from completed years of service
      `untaken hours from the part-year` IS A NUMBER @desc Untaken annual leave hours from the partly completed year
      `untaken hours from years completed after the misconduct` IS A NUMBER @desc Of the completed-year hours, those from years completed after the misconduct (0 if not dismissed for misconduct)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many hours of annual leave must be paid out` IS
    CONSIDER `how it ended`
    WHEN `I walked out unlawfully` THEN `untaken hours from completed years`
    WHEN `my employer ended it for my fault` THEN `untaken hours from completed years`
    WHEN `my employer ended it for my misconduct` THEN `untaken hours from completed years` MINUS `untaken hours from years completed after the misconduct`
    OTHERWISE `untaken hours from completed years` PLUS `untaken hours from the part-year`

§§ `s 27 and s 3 -- paid bereavement leave`

-- s 27(1): "on the death of a member of an employee's family or household the employee is entitled to paid
-- bereavement leave of up to 2 days." (2) "need not be consecutive". s 3: member of the employee's family or
-- household means "(a) the employee's spouse or de facto partner; (b) a child, stepchild or grandchild of the employee
-- or the employee's spouse or de facto partner ...; (c) a parent, step-parent or grandparent ...; (d) a sibling ...;
-- (e) any other person who ... lived with the employee as a member of the employee's household". s 27 is not limited
-- to non-casual employees (s 18(5) contemplates casual loading in bereavement pay).
DECLARE WhoDied IS ONE OF
    `my spouse or de facto partner`
    `my or my partner's child, stepchild or grandchild`
    `my or my partner's parent, step-parent or grandparent`
    `my or my partner's sibling`
    `someone who lived with me as a member of my household`
    `another relative or a friend who did not live with me`

@export How many days of paid bereavement leave do I get (s 27)?
GIVEN `who died` IS A WhoDied @desc Who died, in relation to you
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days of bereavement leave` IS
    CONSIDER `who died`
    WHEN `another relative or a friend who did not live with me` THEN 0
    OTHERWISE 2

§§ `ss 30, 31 -- public holidays`

-- s 30(1): "An employee is entitled to be absent from work on a day or part of a day that is a public holiday." (2) the
-- employer may request work "if the request is reasonable"; (3) "the employee may refuse the request if (a) the
-- request is not reasonable; or (b) the refusal is reasonable". Reasonableness is a judgment on the s 30(4) factors:
-- asked as facts.
@export May I refuse a request to work on a public holiday (s 30)?
GIVEN `if the request is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request is reasonable, weighing the s 30(4) factors (the business, your work, your circumstances, your pay, notice)
      `if my refusal is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your refusal is reasonable, weighing the same factors
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I refuse to work the public holiday` IF
        (NOT `if the request is reasonable`)
    OR  `if my refusal is reasonable`

-- s 31(1): applies to an employee absent on a public holiday "other than (a) a casual employee; or (b) an employee who
-- would not ordinarily work on the public holiday; or (c) an employee who is on unpaid leave on the public holiday."
-- (2) paid "as if the employee were required to work their ordinary hours".
@export Am I paid for a public holiday I take off (s 31)?
GIVEN `I am casual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a casual employee
      `I would ordinarily work that day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would ordinarily work on that day
      `I am on unpaid leave that day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are on unpaid leave that day
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I paid for the public holiday` IF
        (NOT `I am casual`)
    AND `I would ordinarily work that day`
    AND (NOT `I am on unpaid leave that day`)

§§ `ss 39B, 39C -- unpaid family and domestic violence leave`

-- s 39B(1): "5 days of unpaid family and domestic violence leave in a 12-month period"; (2)(c) "available in full to
-- part-time and casual employees". s 39C: may be taken if "(a) the employee is experiencing family and domestic
-- violence; and (b) the employee needs to do something to deal with the impact ...; and (c) it is impractical for the
-- employee to do that thing outside the employee's ordinary hours of work."
@export How many days of unpaid family and domestic violence leave can I take now (ss 39B, 39C)?
GIVEN `I am experiencing family and domestic violence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are experiencing family violence (Restraining Orders Act 1997 s 5A)
      `I need to do something to deal with its impact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you need to do something to deal with its impact
      `I cannot practically do it outside my ordinary hours` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is impractical to do it outside your ordinary hours of work
      `days already taken in this 12-month period` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of this leave already taken in the current 12-month period of your employment
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days of family violence leave can I take` IS
    IF `I am experiencing family and domestic violence`
       AND `I need to do something to deal with its impact`
       AND `I cannot practically do it outside my ordinary hours`
    THEN max 0 (5 MINUS `days already taken in this 12-month period`)
    ELSE 0

§§ `ss 40, 41, 43 -- redundancy`

-- s 40(1): in Part 5 "employee does not include a casual employee or an apprentice"; "redundant means being no longer
-- required by an employer to continue doing a job because the employer has decided that the job will not be done by
-- any person". s 41(1): where the employer has decided to make an employee redundant (or take action with a
-- significant effect), "the employee is entitled to be informed by the employer, as soon as reasonably practicable
-- after the decision has been made" and to discuss its likely effects and measures to avoid or minimise them.
@export Am I entitled to be told of the redundancy and to discuss it with my employer (s 41)?
GIVEN `I am casual or an apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a casual employee or an apprentice
      `my employer has decided no one will do my job` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer has decided your job will not be done by anyone
      `my employer has decided on a change with a significant effect on me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer has decided on a major workforce change, loss of job or promotion opportunity, a big change in hours, retraining, transfer or restructure of your job (s 40(2))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I entitled to be told and consulted` IF
        (NOT `I am casual or an apprentice`)
    AND (`my employer has decided no one will do my job` OR `my employer has decided on a change with a significant effect on me`)

-- s 43(1): "An employee, other than a seasonal worker who has been informed that the employee has been, or will be,
-- made redundant is entitled to paid leave of up to 8 hours for the purpose of being interviewed for further
-- employment." (2) "need not be consecutive". READING: "other than a seasonal worker" qualifies the employee, and
-- "who has been informed" the whole.
@export How many hours of paid leave can I take for job interviews after being told I am redundant (s 43)?
GIVEN `I am casual or an apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a casual employee or an apprentice
      `I am a seasonal worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a seasonal worker
      `I have been told I am or will be made redundant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer has told you that you have been, or will be, made redundant
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many hours of job interview leave` IS
    IF (NOT `I am casual or an apprentice`) AND (NOT `I am a seasonal worker`) AND `I have been told I am or will be made redundant`
    THEN 8
    ELSE 0
